  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/7c49fb8a3f3de48fa6c123db63bbccf20d0e63d8fdac74e8889fef643406d758.png)\n\nWhich of the polyhedra above are convex?",
  "worked_solution": "A polyhedron is convex if and only if the following condition holds:\n\n*If we take any two distinct points $A$ and $B$ that belong to the polyhedron, then all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must also belong to the polyhedron.*  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our solids in turn.\n\n- Solids I and II are convex. Indeed, the above condition holds for any two points that belong to the solids.\n\n- Solid III is *not* convex. The above condition does not hold. As a counterexample, let's consider the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/c4825f7162d9dd689e03c44e17e36f67201df1d7101cd8b795ff2108f1375932.png)\n\n Notice that some points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ lie outside the solid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
